BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 620.30 Td (Imagine a distributed system with two clusters located in different regions: Asia\(the cluster on the left\) and the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 606.90 Td (USA\(the cluster on the right\). Under normal conditions, they sync perfectly. But then, a submarine cable is) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 593.50 Td (damaged, and the two regions lose connection. They are now \223partitioned.\224) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 573.60 Td (A user in Asia updates their profile. Since the Asia cluster cannot talk to the USA cluster, we have to make a) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 560.20 Td (design choice:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 540.30 Td (The CP Choice \(Consistency\): We want the USA user to see the updated data. But because the network is) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 526.90 Td (broken, we can\222t sync the update. To maintain consistency, we must return an error to the USA user) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 513.50 Td (\(decreasing Availability\). We\222d rather show a \223Service Unavailable\224 message than show the wrong data.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 493.60 Td (The AP Choice \(Availability\): We want the system to have zero downtime. Even though the clusters can\222t talk,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 480.20 Td (the USA cluster still responds to the user. However, the user sees the old, stale data because the Asia update) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 466.80 Td (hasn\222t arrived yet. The system is available, but the data is inconsistent.) Tj ET
BT /F2 14.5 Tf 58.00 430.90 Td (Which one should you choose ?) Tj ET
