BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 778.00 Td (Imagine we have an array of 128 buckets where each bucket consists of a single bit that is set to zero. Since it\222s) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 764.60 Td (a bit, it can only have two values: either 0 or 1. Initially, all 128 bits are set to 0.) Tj ET
q 479.00 0 0 75.04 58.00 664.66 cm /Im45 Do Q
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 655.66 Td (Let\222s consider the previous example itself, the example of finding a username.. \(for better understanding let\222s) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 642.26 Td (say we already have 3 users \223pushu\224, \223giri\224 and \223vikram\224, and we have marked their hash values to 1.\)) Tj ET
q 479.00 0 0 75.04 58.00 542.31 cm /Im46 Do Q
BT /F2 10.5 Tf 58.00 522.31 Td (Insert Operation) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 504.66 Td (When the user is typing a username, we might do a GET availability check in the bloom filter \(refer to the query) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 491.26 Td (operation section below\).) Tj ET
q 479.00 0 0 213.95 58.00 252.41 cm /Im47 Do Q
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 243.41 Td (But the insert happens when the bloom filter query operation \(GET\) returns a 0 \(100% certain that the user is not) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 230.01 Td (present\) and only when the user finally clicks Sign Up and we create the account via a POST request. Only after) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 216.61 Td (the DB insert succeeds, we update the Bloom filter like below.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 196.71 Td (For insert operation we need to perform the following operations :) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 176.81 Td (Take the hash value of the username) Tj ET
