BT /F2 10.5 Tf 58.00 539.05 Td (Query/Read Operation) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 521.40 Td (For query operation \(meaning to see if a username exists or not \)we perform the following steps :) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 501.50 Td (Take the hash value of the username we are checking) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 481.60 Td (mod it with the total number of bits in the array : hash\(username\) % 128 in our case) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 461.70 Td (then we check if the bit at that index is set or not.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 441.80 Td (If the index is not set \(meaning it is 0\), then it is 100% certain that the username is not registered. But if the index) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 428.40 Td (is set to 1, then it means that the username may or may not be registered, so we have to make that db call to) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 415.00 Td (know if it really exists or no.) Tj ET
q 479.00 0 0 202.24 58.00 187.85 cm /Im49 Do Q
BT /F2 10.5 Tf 58.00 167.85 Td (Some Important Points to Note) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 150.20 Td (In the query operation, the \223may or may not be present\224 case introduces false positivity, meaning bloom filters) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 136.80 Td (can say taken \(the set bit \2211\222\) even when it\222s actually not taken \(so we are hitting the db for such cases\). This is) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 123.40 Td (called the false positivity rate, p.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 103.50 Td (Number of keys we put in bloom filters increase, the false positivity rate increases) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 83.60 Td (In the above example, we considered only 1 hash function. But in real systems, we use k hash functions,) Tj ET
