BT /F2 12 Tf 58.00 366.45 Td (Kafka Broker) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 345.85 Td (Kafka brokers are basically the servers that run Kafka. We talked about how producers publish data to a topic,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 332.45 Td (and consumers consume from the topic. We also talked about how the data is appended into an append-only log) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 319.05 Td (inside partitions. But where does all of this actually live and run?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 299.15 Td (That\222s where brokers come in.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 279.25 Td (When a producer publishes an event to a topic, Kafka automatically routes that event to the correct partition of) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 265.85 Td (that topic. That partition is stored on some broker, and the broker is responsible for receiving the write request) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 252.45 Td (and persisting the event to disk. Similarly, when a consumer subscribes to a topic, it reads the data by fetching) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 239.05 Td (messages from the brokers that host the partitions for that topic.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 219.15 Td (A Kafka cluster is simply a group of multiple brokers working together. Having multiple brokers allows Kafka to) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 205.75 Td (distribute partitions across machines, handle high traffic, and stay available, even if one broker goes down. So) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 192.35 Td (by adding more brokers, Kafka can scale horizontally without changing producer or consumer code.) Tj ET
