BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 460.10 Td (Question : if a topic has multiple partitions and you spin up multiple consumers, how does Kafka decide who) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 446.70 Td (reads what, and how does it avoid two consumers accidentally processing the same message \(and avoid) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 433.30 Td (duplicate processing while preserving ordering\)?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 413.40 Td (Answer:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 393.50 Td (If consumers were allowed to read partitions independently without any coordination, then every consumer could) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 380.10 Td (end up reading the same partitions and hence the same messages, leading to duplicate processing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 360.20 Td (Kafka solves this by introducing consumer groups. A consumer group is just a logical grouping of consumers) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 346.80 Td (identified by the same group.id. When multiple consumers join the same group, Kafka treats them like a team) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 333.40 Td (that wants to share the work, and it enforces a very important rule:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 313.50 Td (each partition is assigned to at most one consumer within the group) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 293.60 Td (A consumer can read from one or more partitions, but no two consumers in the same group will read from the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 280.20 Td (same partition. This keeps ordering intact \(since each partition is an ordered log\) and ensures that each) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 266.80 Td (message is processed only once per consumer group. \(but different consumers in different consumer groups) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 253.40 Td (can consume from those partitions.. \).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 233.50 Td (So since consumers in a consumer group are consuming from partitions, here\222s how they are related.:) Tj ET
