q 479.00 0 0 366.70 58.00 406.30 cm /Im58 Do Q
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 397.30 Td (If number of consumers in a consumer group < number of partitions of the topic it is consuming from -> each) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 383.90 Td (consumer is assigned multiple partitions) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 364.00 Td (If number of consumers in a consumer group == number of partitions of the topic it is consuming from -> each) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 350.60 Td (consumer is assigned exactly one partition) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 330.70 Td (If number of consumers in a consumer group > number of partitions of the topic it is consuming from -> extra) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 317.30 Td (consumers remain idle \(for that topic\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 297.40 Td (If a consumer group subscribes to multiple topics -> this assignment logic is applied independently per topic) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 277.50 Td (Now remember the offset we briefly introduced earlier as the position number \(0, 1, 2\205\) of a message inside a) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 264.10 Td (partition, this is exactly why offsets are useful. As consumers read from a partition, Kafka tracks how far they) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 250.70 Td (have read using offsets, and these offsets are tracked per consumer group, per partition. Kafka stores these) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 237.30 Td (committed offsets inside an internal Kafka topic called __consumer_offsets, which is how Kafka can remember) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 223.90 Td (progress even if a consumer crashes or restarts. Because of this, consumers can resume from where they left) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 210.50 Td (off after failures, and different consumer groups can consume the same topic independently with their own) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 197.10 Td (progress) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 177.20 Td (I will dedicate a seperate article to discuss on the internals of kafka Consumers) Tj ET
0.84 g 58.00 151.60 479.00 0.70 re f
0 g
BT /F2 12 Tf 58.00 130.30 Td (Replication) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 109.70 Td (Till now, we talked about how partitions store data on brokers. But if a partition existed on only one broker, Kafka) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 96.30 Td (would not be fault tolerant,, if that broker went down, data would be unavailable or even lost right. Kafka solves) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 82.90 Td (this using replication.) Tj ET
