BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 778.00 Td (Leader Replica: Each partition has one replica elected as the leader, and that leader lives on a broker.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 764.60 Td (Producers and consumers always talk to the leader for that partition \(writes and reads happen via the leader\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 744.70 Td (Follower Replicas: The same partition is replicated onto other brokers as followers. Followers don\222t serve client) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 731.30 Td (traffic, they passively replicate data from the leader.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 711.40 Td (Distributed Leadership: In the architecture diagram above, notice how leaders are spread across brokers \(ex:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 698.00 Td (Partition A-0 leader on Broker 1, Partition A-1 leader on Broker 2\). This is intentional so read/write load is) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 684.60 Td (distributed and no single broker becomes a hotspot.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 664.70 Td (ISR \(In-Sync Replicas\): Followers that are fully caught up with the leader are part of the ISR. ISR is critical) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 651.30 Td (because only an in-sync follower can safely be promoted as the next leader without losing acknowledged data.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 631.40 Td (Failover: If the broker hosting a partition leader goes down, Kafka promotes one of the ISR followers as the new) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 618.00 Td (leader, so producers and consumers can continue operating with minimal downtime. When the failed broker) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 604.60 Td (returns, it usually rejoins as a follower and starts replicating again.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 584.70 Td (At this point, we understand what replication is and why Kafka uses leaders, followers, and ISR. But this) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 571.30 Td (naturally raises the next question: who tracks broker liveness, who decides leaders, and who coordinates) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 557.90 Td (failover and metadata updates? That responsibility belongs to Kafka\222s control plane the KRaft Controller.) Tj ET
0.84 g 58.00 532.30 479.00 0.70 re f
0 g
BT /F2 12 Tf 58.00 511.00 Td (KRaft Controller) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 490.40 Td (Kafka replication and failover only work if the cluster has a single, consistent authority that coordinates metadata) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 477.00 Td (changes. That role is handled by the Kafka Controller \(Kafka\222s control plane\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 457.10 Td (At a high level, the controller\222s responsibilities include:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 437.20 Td (Leader assignment: deciding which replica is the leader for each partition) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 417.30 Td (Managing replication state: tracking replicas and maintaining the ISR set) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 397.40 Td (Broker health monitoring: detecting when brokers go down or come back up) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 377.50 Td (Failover coordination: when a leader broker fails, promoting an in-sync follower as the new leader so the partition) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 364.10 Td (stays available) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 344.20 Td (Earlier versions of Kafka used ZooKeeper for this coordination. Modern Kafka replaced ZooKeeper with KRaft) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 330.80 Td (\(Kafka Raft\), where Kafka manages this metadata internally \(no external dependency\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 310.90 Td (One important note: the controller doesn\222t handle data traffic, producers and consumers don\222t talk to KRaft) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 297.50 Td (directly. Kraft just coordinates the cluster/brokers in the background so Kafka keeps working correctly even as) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 284.10 Td (brokers come and go.) Tj ET
0.84 g 58.00 258.50 479.00 0.70 re f
0 g
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 250.20 Td (Well, this article was covering the fundamentals of Kafka, In the coming months, we\222ll go deeper into the parts) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 236.80 Td (that matter when you actually run Kafka in production:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 216.90 Td (Kafka Producer Internals : Deep dive into the Producer API) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 197.00 Td (Kafka Consumer Internals : Deep dive into how Consumer API works) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 177.10 Td (End to End delivery semantics : At-least-once vs at-most-once vs exactly-once, how duplicates happen, how) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 163.70 Td (idempotency and transactions work, and what guarantees Kafka can \(and cannot\) provide.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 143.80 Td (Thanks for reading Evolving Engineer! Subscribe for free to receive new posts and support my work.) Tj ET
